# Session entry point: ephemeral by design, rewritten each session.
START_HERE.md

# In-process and working files, including the message a commit is given with
# -F. Gitignored so that naming it on the command line can never put it into
# the commit it describes.
.ephemera/

# Run directories. A tree accumulating them would otherwise feed each run the
# last one's evidence, and only THIS run's output is excluded structurally.
.bolt-*

# The installed binary. `~/bin/bolt` resolves HERE as of 2026-08-29, so this is
# what every gate in the estate runs.
#
# Ignored rather than committed, which is what bolt.go did before it: a built
# artefact is not source, and committing 6MB per rebuild would make the history
# the binary's rather than the code's. Install it with
#
#     cargo build --release && cp target/release/bolt bin/bolt
#
# and note that nothing rebuilds it for you. A stale `bin/bolt` answers as of
# when it was built, which is `docs/LESSONS/a-second-build-answers-for-the-tree.md`
# with the whole estate downstream instead of one session.
/bin/bolt
target/
Cargo.lock

# The adopted jig links. `toolbox/jigs.yaml` declares the set and `link-toolbox`
# makes them, so adoption is a step a clone performs rather than content this
# repository ships. In a built image the same files arrive from the layer
# beneath, so a committed copy would be a third statement of one thing.
#
# A tracked symlink stores its target path as content, so committing one encodes
# where ../toolbox happens to sit and dangles in any clone without that sibling.
#
# MATCHED BY SHAPE RATHER THAN NAMED ONE AT A TIME. `bolt.*-quality.yaml` is
# narrow enough to be safe: it cannot match a `*.definitions.yaml`, and this
# repository carries no jig of its own in any case, since `bolt.rust-quality.yaml`
# moved to toolbox on 2026-09-03.
#
# THE LIST IS WHAT WENT WRONG. Adopting toolbox's `rust` set added
# `bolt.rust-std-quality.yaml` and `config/rust/clippy.toml`; neither was added
# here, so both sat untracked where `git add -A` would have committed them as
# symlinks — the exact failure the paragraph above describes. A pattern covers a
# set that grows and a list does not.
#
# `bin/count-selection.py` was the other reason given for naming files here and
# it no longer exists, so `bin/` now holds nothing of this project's but the
# built binary named above.
bolt.*-quality.yaml
bolt.secrets.yaml
bin/*.py
adapters/
config/
